From eaee068d59b1d44277dd486cee43daacc146436a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 04:28:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Fix Postgres transaction poisoning, multi-user data
 collisions, and add health monitoring
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Database layer (database.py)
- PostgresWrapper.execute() never rolled back on error. Postgres aborts the
  whole transaction on any failure, so the first swallowed error silently broke
  every later statement on that connection. Concretely: init_db()'s idempotent
  ALTER TABLE migrations always fail on the first one (ai_brief is already in
  the CREATE TABLE), which then blocked portfolio.user_id, watchlist.user_id
  and the ai_predictions table from ever being created. Verified against a real
  Postgres 16: before the fix later migrations raise InFailedSqlTransaction.
- Added a public rollback(); both backends now expose it.
- INSERT OR REPLACE translation matched only a spacing variant the callers do
  not use, and spliced ON CONFLICT in before VALUES (invalid SQL). Push
  subscribe therefore always failed on Postgres. Now matched by table name and
  appended after VALUES.
- Accept "postgres://" as well as "postgresql://" — the former silently fell
  back to SQLite on ephemeral disk, losing all data on redeploy.

Multi-user correctness (app.py, alerts.py)
- portfolio/watchlist were UNIQUE(ticker) while rows are per-user, so the
  second user to add a ticker hit a constraint violation (500 on watchlist,
  silent data loss on portfolio). Now UNIQUE(ticker, user_id), with migrations
  that swap the constraint on existing databases without losing rows.
- /api/alerts returned every user's alerts, including portfolio P/L messages.
  Alerts now carry user_id and are filtered on read and clear.

Crashes and data loss
- /seed was a GET that deletes the day's real scan results; now POST-only and
  gated behind ALLOW_SEED.
- /earnings had no guard around get_earnings_calendar, whose as_completed
  timeout raises from the iterator — a 500 whenever Yahoo is slow or blocked.
- Dashboard AI brief used .get() on a row, which raises on SQLite and was
  swallowed by a bare except.

Scoring and display
- Morningstar (18-point weight) could never fire: fundamentals were only read
  from an .info fetch that is skipped whenever yahoo_data supplies a price, so
  ms_score capped at 2 against a threshold of 3. Now passed through from data
  already fetched, at no extra request cost.
- Market regime was computed every scan and never written to the DB.
- Daily email rendered "$None" for every price when yfinance is blocked.
- print_results crashed on abs(None) when the S&P quote was rate-limited.
- Charts, sector heatmap and history bars were scaled to the dead 0-100 axis.
- Checklist and analyze progress counted 13 items when 14 exist ("14 of 13",
  108% bar).
- Analyze page fed a 0-100 analyst score into thresholds calibrated for the
  ~30-point convergence scale, printing "Strong Buy" for Hold-rated stocks.
- SECTOR_MAP key was BRK.B while the universe uses BRK-B.
- CLI conviction bands realigned to the 25/15 tiers used everywhere else.

Reliability
- Service worker was registered from /static/sw.js, whose scope controls no
  page, so navigator.serviceWorker.ready never resolved and push subscription
  hung forever. Now uses the root-scoped /sw.js route that already existed.
- Connection leaks closed on the alerts and price-alert error paths.

Monitoring (new: health.py, monitor.py, MONITORING.md)
- GET /health self-diagnoses from inside the deployment: database, scan
  freshness and quality, price coverage, backtest output, upstream reachability,
  config presence, and renders every page server-side. 503 when critical.
- monitor.py drives the live site from outside: logs in, loads every page,
  compares stored prices against an independent source, and can ask Claude to
  diagnose failures. Exit codes suit cron.
- Both were validated by reintroducing two real bugs and confirming detection.
